  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1122 PM CDT TUE AUG 4 2026\n\nA mid to upper level low will continue to slide northeastward\nthrough northern Georgia. Drier air will remain across the west,\nwhile better moisture will be in place across the east, closer to\nthe upper low and its wrap-around. This will help maintain a 20-\n60% chance for showers and storms again Wednesday\nafternoon/evening. By Thursday, the low will begin to drift\nnorth, eventually being absorbed by another approaching upper\nlevel trough moving across the Ohio Valley. A more typical\nsummertime pattern sets up the weekend with weak flow aloft,\nleading to diurnally driven showers and thunderstorms into early\nnext week.\n\nPatchy fog is anticipated near sunrise Wednesday morning as dew\npoint depressions decrease. Visibility restrictions are most\nlikely in areas that received rainfall this afternoon.\n\nTemperatures will trend warmer through the week, with highs\nreaching the low to mid 90s through the weekend. High confidence\nexists that humid conditions will push max heat indices to 95-\n100 degrees, creating a moderate heat risk across Central\nAlabama.\n\n16",
